---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Analog Temperature Sensor">

  <section class="prose max-w-none">

    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
      Analog Temperature Sensor
    </h1>

    <!-- Project Description -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Project Description
    </h2>

    <p>
      This project is a fully analog temperature sensor designed using
      diode-connected BJTs and precision analog circuitry. Two matched BJTs are
      biased at different current levels, producing a temperature-dependent
      voltage difference that is amplified and scaled to create an output
      directly proportional to temperature in degrees Fahrenheit.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two sensor currents are generated from a common reference branch using
      a current mirror and Widlar current source. This establishes a controlled
      current ratio between the sensing transistors while allowing both devices
      to operate from the same reference.
    </p>

    <p>
      The resulting voltage difference is processed by a differential amplifier
      and scaled so that a 1 mV increase in output voltage corresponds to a
      1 °F increase in temperature. Particular attention was given to reducing
      errors caused by op-amp offset voltage, offset current, and input bias
      currents, resulting in an estimated amplifier error of approximately
      0.86 mV.
    </p>


    <!-- How it Works -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      How it Works
    </h2>

    <p>
      The temperature measurement is based on the relationship between a BJT's
      base-emitter voltage, collector current, and absolute temperature. Rather
      than using the base-emitter voltage of a single transistor as the
      temperature signal, the circuit compares two matched diode-connected BJTs
      operating at different currents.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two sensing transistors are biased using currents derived from the
      same reference branch. A current mirror establishes one sensor current,
      while a Widlar current source generates a lower current for the second
      transistor. The ratio between these currents determines the sensitivity
      of the differential sensor voltage.
    </p>

    <p>
      Subtracting the two junction voltages removes the saturation-current term
      shared by the matched devices and leaves a voltage proportional to
      absolute temperature. The sensor therefore produces a small differential
      voltage that changes approximately linearly with temperature.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two sensor voltages, V1 and V2, are passed into a differential
      amplifier. The amplifier scales the small temperature-dependent
      difference while a reference voltage shifts the output so that it
      directly represents temperature in degrees Fahrenheit.
    </p>


    <!-- Design Equations -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Design Equations
    </h2>

    <p>
      For two matched diode-connected BJTs operating at different collector
      currents, subtracting their base-emitter voltages gives the
      temperature-dependent sensor voltage:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      V<sub>s</sub> =
      ΔV<sub>BE</sub> =
      (kT / q) ln(I<sub>1</sub> / I<sub>2</sub>)
    </div>

    <p>
      The temperature sensitivity is therefore set by the ratio between the two
      BJT bias currents. For a desired sensor slope <em>a</em> in volts per
      degree Fahrenheit, the required current ratio is determined from:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      a =
      (5k / 9q)
      ln(I<sub>1</sub> / I<sub>2</sub>)
    </div>

    <p>
      After selecting one sensor current, this relationship determines the
      second current required to produce the desired temperature sensitivity.
      The two currents are generated from the same reference branch using a
      current mirror and Widlar current source.
    </p>

    <p>
      The reference resistor is selected from the total current required by the
      bias network and the available supply voltage:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      R<sub>ref</sub> =
      (|V<sub>SS</sub>| - V<sub>BE</sub>) /
      I<sub>ref</sub>
    </div>

    <p>
      The emitter resistor in the Widlar source establishes the reduced current
      in the second sensor branch. Its value is determined from the
      base-emitter voltage difference produced by the selected current ratio:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      R<sub>Widlar</sub> =
      V<sub>T</sub>
      ln(I<sub>1</sub> / I<sub>2</sub>) /
      I<sub>E2</sub>
    </div>

    <p>
      These component values establish the two sensor bias currents directly
      from a single reference branch, producing the required
      temperature-dependent differential voltage before amplification.
    </p>

    <p>
      The differential amplifier then scales and offsets the sensor voltage so
      that the final output changes by 1 mV for every 1 °F change in
      temperature:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      V<sub>o</sub> = GV<sub>s</sub> + CV<sub>ref</sub>
    </div>


    <!-- Schematic -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Schematic
    </h2>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/TempSensor.png"
        alt="Analog temperature sensor schematic"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The left side of the schematic generates the two temperature-dependent
      sensor voltages. The sensing BJTs are biased from a shared reference
      branch, with a current mirror producing one bias current and a Widlar
      source producing the second.
    </p>

    <p>
      The right side contains the differential amplification stages. The small
      voltage difference generated by the sensor is amplified while a reference
      voltage establishes the output offset required for direct conversion to
      degrees Fahrenheit.
    </p>


    <!-- Error Reduction -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Error Reduction
    </h2>

    <p>
      Since the raw voltage generated by the BJT pair is small, op-amp
      nonidealities can produce errors comparable to the signal being measured.
      The amplifier was therefore designed using nonideal op-amp models rather
      than assuming ideal behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      Input bias-current error was addressed by matching the equivalent
      resistance seen by the two inputs of each amplifier. The compensating
      resistors are chosen from the parallel combinations of the corresponding
      feedback and input resistors, causing the voltage drops created by equal
      input bias currents to cancel.
    </p>

    <p>
      After this cancellation, the remaining amplifier error is primarily
      determined by the input offset voltage and input offset current. The
      resulting output error can be expressed as:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      V<sub>o,error</sub> =
      2GV<sub>OS</sub> +
      I<sub>OS</sub>R<sub>2</sub>
    </div>

    <p>
      The op-amp and resistor values were selected to minimize this error while
      maintaining the required temperature gain. For the final amplifier
      design, the calculated output error is approximately 0.86 mV, corresponding
      to less than approximately one degree Fahrenheit at the 1 mV/°F output
      sensitivity.
    </p>


    <!-- Results and Validation -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Results and Validation
    </h2>

    <p>
      The completed circuit was simulated across the intended temperature range
      to verify both the output scaling and the effect of circuit
      nonidealities. The resulting transfer characteristic remains linear,
      allowing the output voltage to be interpreted directly as temperature.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/TempTransfer.png"
        alt="Analog temperature sensor transfer plot"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      In addition to the nominal transfer characteristic, the circuit was
      simulated with the modeled op-amp errors included. Comparing the nonideal
      output against the ideal response provides a direct measurement of the
      error introduced by the amplification stages.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/TempError.png"
        alt="Analog temperature sensor output error plot"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Individual operating points were also checked against the theoretical
      transfer relationship to verify the circuit at specific temperatures.
      These values provide an additional comparison between the analytical
      design and the simulated circuit response.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/TempValidation.png"
        alt="Analog temperature sensor validation points"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>


    <!-- Design Result -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Design Result
    </h2>

    <p>
      The completed signal chain converts the small temperature-dependent
      voltage generated by the BJT pair into an easily interpreted analog
      output. Increasing the temperature by 1 °F produces an approximately
      1 mV increase in output voltage, allowing temperature to be determined
      directly from the measured voltage.
    </p>

    <p>
      The project combines transistor-level temperature sensing, current-mirror
      design, a Widlar current source, differential amplification, and nonideal
      op-amp analysis to create a complete analog sensing system. The resulting
      circuit demonstrates how device-level transistor behavior can be converted
      into a practical and accurately scaled measurement.
    </p>


    <style>
      .project-figure {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
      }

      .project-image {
        width: 100%;
        display: block;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        background: #f5f5f5;
      }

      .project-figure figcaption {
        margin-top: 0.5rem;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        opacity: 0.7;
      }

      .ik-diagram {
        max-width: 400px;
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
      }

      .video-figure {
        max-width: 850px;
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
      }

      .project-video {
        width: 100%;
        max-height: 650px;
        display: block;
        object-fit: contain;
        background: #111;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
      }

      .equation {
        margin: 1.5rem auto;
        padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
        width: fit-content;
        max-width: 100%;
        text-align: center;
        font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        border-radius: 8px;
        background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08);
      }
    </style>

  </section>

</BaseLayout>